.TH NP_PACKET 3 "June 2026" "netpipe v0.1.0" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
np_packet_alloc, np_packet_free, np_packet_clone, np_packet_t, np_layer_t, np_proto_t, np_http_msg_t, np_dns_msg_t \- netpipe packet structures and lifecycle
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <netpipe.h>
.PP
.BI "np_packet_t *np_packet_alloc(size_t " caplen ");"
.br
.BI "void np_packet_free(np_packet_t *" pkt ");"
.br
.BI "np_packet_t *np_packet_clone(const np_packet_t *" src ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
These functions and structures represent and manage individual network packets in the netpipe library.
.SS Packet Lifecycle
.PP
The
.BR np_packet_alloc ()
function allocates a new packet structure with space for raw captured packet bytes up to
.I caplen
size.
It returns a pointer to the newly allocated packet or
.B NULL
on failure.
.PP
The
.BR np_packet_free ()
function releases all memory associated with the packet, including internal scratch space and reassembled stream data.
.PP
The
.BR np_packet_clone ()
function creates a complete deep copy of the packet
.IR src .
It returns a pointer to the cloned packet or
.B NULL
on failure.
.SS Structures
.PP
A packet is represented by the
.B np_packet_t
structure:
.PP
.nf
typedef struct np_packet {
    struct timespec  ts;            /* Capture timestamp */
    uint32_t         caplen;        /* Captured length */
    uint32_t         wirelen;       /* Length on the wire */
    uint8_t         *raw;           /* Raw captured frame bytes */
    int              nlayers;       /* Count of active layers */
    np_layer_t       layers[8];     /* Layer list */
    
    /* Convenience pointers to layers (NULL if layer not present) */
    const np_layer_t *eth;          /* Ethernet / Link layer */
    const np_layer_t *net;          /* IPv4 / IPv6 / ARP */
    const np_layer_t *transport;    /* TCP / UDP / ICMP */
    const np_layer_t *app;          /* HTTP / DNS / TLS */

    uint64_t         seq;           /* Capture sequence number */
    uint32_t         flow_id;       /* Flow hash signature */
    uint8_t         *stream_data;   /* TCP stream reassembled payload */
    size_t           stream_len;    /* Reassembled stream length */
} np_packet_t;
.fi
.PP
Each protocol layer inside the packet is described by
.B np_layer_t\fR:
.PP
.nf
typedef struct np_layer {
    np_proto_t    proto;      /* Protocol identifier */
    const uint8_t *data;      /* Pointer to starting byte in raw frame */
    size_t        len;       /* Length of this layer's header + payload */
    void         *decoded;   /* Points to decoded structures (if any) */
} np_layer_t;
.fi
.PP
The protocol identifier
.B np_proto_t
is an enum representing protocols parsed by the demuxer:
.TP
.B NP_PROTO_RAW
Raw IP frame (no Link layer).
.TP
.B NP_PROTO_ETH
Ethernet (Layer 2).
.TP
.B NP_PROTO_ARP
Address Resolution Protocol.
.TP
.B NP_PROTO_IP4
IPv4 (Layer 3).
.TP
.B NP_PROTO_IP6
IPv6 (Layer 3).
.TP
.B NP_PROTO_ICMP
ICMP or ICMPv6 (Layer 4).
.TP
.B NP_PROTO_TCP
TCP (Layer 4).
.TP
.B NP_PROTO_UDP
UDP (Layer 4).
.TP
.B NP_PROTO_DNS
Domain Name System (Layer 7).
The layer's
.I decoded
pointer points to a
.B np_dns_msg_t
structure.
.TP
.B NP_PROTO_HTTP
Hypertext Transfer Protocol (Layer 7).
The layer's
.I decoded
pointer points to a
.B np_http_msg_t
structure.
.TP
.B NP_PROTO_TLS
Transport Layer Security (Layer 7).
.SS Decoded Protocol Objects
For protocols with high-level parsing enabled (DNS, HTTP), the demuxer populates structures in packet-scoped scratch memory.
These are zero-copy structures, meaning strings do not allocate heap memory but point directly back to offsets in the packet's raw frame or scratch space.
.PP
A zero-copy string is represented by the
.B np_str_t
structure:
.PP
.nf
typedef struct {
    const char *str;
    size_t      len;
} np_str_t;
.fi
.PP
The decoded HTTP message is represented by the
.B np_http_msg_t
structure:
.PP
.nf
typedef struct {
    bool is_request;                /* Request vs Response */
    
    /* Request specific fields */
    np_str_t method;                /* GET, POST, etc. */
    np_str_t path;                  /* Request URI */
    np_str_t version;               /* HTTP version string */
    
    /* Response specific fields */
    int      status_code;           /* HTTP status code (e.g. 200) */
    np_str_t status_phrase;         /* "OK", "Not Found", etc. */
    
    int              num_headers;
    np_http_header_t headers[32];   /* Name-value header list */
    
    const uint8_t   *body;          /* Message body pointer */
    size_t           body_len;      /* Length of message body */
} np_http_msg_t;
.fi
.PP
The decoded DNS message is represented by the
.B np_dns_msg_t
structure:
.PP
.nf
typedef struct {
    uint16_t id;                    /* DNS query ID */
    bool     is_response;           /* Query vs Response */
    int      rcode;                 /* Response status (e.g. NXDOMAIN) */
    
    char     query_name[256];       /* Queried domain name */
    uint16_t query_type;            /* Query type (A, AAAA, CNAME) */
    
    int             num_answers;
    np_dns_answer_t answers[8];     /* Answer records list */
} np_dns_msg_t;
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUES
.BR np_packet_alloc ()
and
.BR np_packet_clone ()
return a pointer to the allocated packet on success, or
.B NULL
on failure.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR libnetpipe (3),
.BR np_pipeline (3)
